### PR Review: fix: lazy-import embeddings to avoid onnxruntime crash on Node v24+

- [x] Gather context and understand request
- [x] Read changed files
- [x] Analyze code changes
- [x] Post review feedback

---

**Overall**: The approach is correct — converting static imports to dynamic `import()` calls is the right fix for the startup crash. `analyze.ts` and the `api.ts` search path are handled well. However, there is one significant bug that partially undermines the fix for MCP server users.

---

### `gitnexus/src/cli/analyze.ts` — ✅ Correct

The lazy import of `runEmbeddingPipeline` at line 261 is correctly placed inside the `if (!embeddingSkipped)` block. This means `@huggingface/transformers` (and `onnxruntime-node`) are never loaded unless `--embeddings` is explicitly passed and the node count is within limits. This is the cleanest of the three changes.

---

### `gitnexus/src/server/api.ts` — ✅ Mostly correct, minor concern

```typescript
// api.ts:233
const { isEmbedderReady } = await import('../core/embeddings/embedder.js');
if (isEmbedderReady()) {
    const { semanticSearch } = await import('../core/embeddings/embedding-pipeline.js');
    return hybridSearch(query, limit, executeQuery, semanticSearch);
}
```

The logic is correct — `semanticSearch` is only imported when the embedder is ready. However, `isEmbedderReady` itself is imported from `embedder.js`, which has a **top-level static import** of `@huggingface/transformers`:

```typescript
// core/embeddings/embedder.ts:17
import { pipeline, env, type FeatureExtractionPipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';
```

This means the very first search request will still load `onnxruntime-node`, and on Node v24 would crash the server. Since the stated goal is fixing startup crashes (not runtime crashes), this is acceptable — but worth documenting. A future improvement could extract `isEmbedderReady` into a separate lightweight module that doesn't carry the full `@huggingface/transformers` import.

---

### `gitnexus/src/mcp/local/local-backend.ts` — ⚠️ Bug: crash still occurs on graceful shutdown

```typescript
// local-backend.ts:1593-1600
async disconnect(): Promise<void> {
    await closeKuzu();
    const { disposeEmbedder } = await import('../core/embedder.js');  // ← problem
    await disposeEmbedder();
    this.repos.clear();
    ...
}
```

`../core/embedder.js` resolves to `gitnexus/src/mcp/core/embedder.ts`, which has:

```typescript
// mcp/core/embedder.ts:8
import { pipeline, env, type FeatureExtractionPipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';
```

Since `disconnect()` is called unconditionally on **every graceful shutdown** (SIGINT/SIGTERM via `process.once('SIGINT', shutdown)` in `api.ts`), this will:

1. Dynamically import `embedder.js`
2. Node evaluates the module → hits the static `@huggingface/transformers` import
3. `@huggingface/transformers` loads `onnxruntime-node` native binary
4. **Crash on unsupported Node versions** — even if embeddings were never used during the session

This is the exact crash pattern the PR is trying to fix. A user on Node v24 would successfully start the MCP server, use it for queries (without semantic search), then get a crash when `Ctrl-C`ing the server.

**`analyze.ts` already handles this correctly** with the explicit comment at lines 318-320:
> `// Note: we intentionally do NOT call disposeEmbedder() here.`
> `// ONNX Runtime's native cleanup segfaults on macOS and some Linux configs.`
> `// Since the process exits immediately after, Node.js reclaims everything.`

The same rationale applies to `disconnect()` — `process.exit(0)` is called immediately after in the shutdown handler.

**Suggested fix:**

```typescript
async disconnect(): Promise<void> {
    await closeKuzu();
    // Intentionally skip disposeEmbedder() — onnxruntime-node crashes on cleanup
    // on unsupported Node versions, and process.exit(0) follows immediately,
    // so the OS reclaims all resources. See #38, #89.
    this.repos.clear();
    this.contextCache.clear();
    this.initializedRepos.clear();
}
```

Or, if cleanup is needed for non-process-exit cases, track whether the module was ever loaded:

```typescript
private embedderLoaded = false;

// In semanticSearch(), after the dynamic import:
this.embedderLoaded = true;

// In disconnect():
if (this.embedderLoaded) {
    const { disposeEmbedder } = await import('../core/embedder.js');
    await disposeEmbedder();
}
```

[Fix this →](https://claude.ai/code?q=In%20gitnexus%2Fsrc%2Fmcp%2Flocal%2Flocal-backend.ts%2C%20the%20disconnect()%20method%20at%20line%201593%20calls%20%60await%20import('.%2F..%2Fcore%2Fembedder.js')%60%20unconditionally.%20This%20loads%20%40huggingface%2Ftransformers%20and%20onnxruntime-node%20on%20every%20graceful%20shutdown%2C%20crashing%20on%20Node%20v24.%20Remove%20the%20disposeEmbedder()%20call%20(it%27s%20not%20needed%20since%20process.exit(0)%20follows%20immediately)%2C%20matching%20the%20pattern%20in%20analyze.ts%20lines%20318-320.%20Branch%3A%20fix%2Flazy-embed-import&repo=abhigyanpatwari/GitNexus)

---

### Summary

| File | Verdict |
|------|---------|
| `cli/analyze.ts` | ✅ Correct |
| `server/api.ts` | ✅ Correct for startup; first search still loads onnxruntime |
| `mcp/local/local-backend.ts` | ⚠️ `disconnect()` unconditionally loads onnxruntime — crash still occurs on graceful shutdown |

The fix in `local-backend.ts:disconnect()` is needed to fully resolve #89 for MCP server users.

