This is a shallow integration. `standard.ts` is supposed to be **language-agnostic generic fallback** — the fact that it now has three Python-specific code blocks (PEP 328 relative imports, the `.rb`-dead-code ternary, and the new proximity block) signals architectural pressure. Every other language with complex import semantics (Go, JVM, C#, PHP, Ruby, Rust) has its **own resolver module** and a **first-class case in `resolveLanguageImport`**. Python does not.

