### Test 2 — EPIPE causes clean exit (code 0)

This requires `spawn` (async) instead of `spawnSync`, so you can close the read end of the pipe while the child is still writing:

```typescript
import { spawn } from 'child_process';

it('cypher: EPIPE exits with code 0, not stderr dump', (done) => {
  // Produce enough output to fill the pipe buffer so EPIPE triggers
  const child = spawn(
    process.execPath,
    ['--import', 'tsx', cliEntry, 'cypher', 'MATCH (n) RETURN n LIMIT 500'],
    {
      cwd: MINI_REPO,
      stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'],
      env: {
        ...process.env,
        NODE_OPTIONS: `${process.env.NODE_OPTIONS || ''} --max-old-space-size=8192`.trim(),
      },
    },
  );

  let stderrOutput = '';
  child.stderr.on('data', (chunk) => { stderrOutput += chunk.toString(); });

  // Destroy stdout immediately — this simulates `| head -0` (consumer closes early)
  child.stdout.once('data', () => {
    child.stdout.destroy(); // triggers EPIPE on the next write
  });

  child.on('close', (code) => {
    // Clean EPIPE exit: code 0, not a stderr dump of the full JSON
    expect(code).toBe(0);
    // No JSON payload should appear on stderr
    expect(() => JSON.parse(stderrOutput.trim())).toThrow();
    done();
  });
}, 20000);
```

**Why this matters:** Before the EPIPE fix, the full JSON payload would be dumped to stderr when the pipe consumer closed early (e.g., `gitnexus cypher ... | head -1`).

