### Review

The implementation is minimal and correct. The logic in `analyze.ts:271–275` is the key change:

```ts
if (stats.nodes > EMBEDDING_NODE_LIMIT && !options.ignoreEmbeddingsNodeLimit) {
  embeddingSkipReason = `skipped (${stats.nodes.toLocaleString()} nodes > ${EMBEDDING_NODE_LIMIT.toLocaleString()} limit; use --ignore-embeddings-node-limit to override)`;
} else {
  embeddingSkipped = false;
}
```

This is clean — no redundancy, no wasted work. Short-circuit logic is handled correctly by the outer `if (options?.embeddings)` guard.

---

### Issues Found

**1. Silent no-op when flag is used without `--embeddings`** _(medium)_

`--ignore-embeddings-node-limit` without `--embeddings` is silently ignored. Users who misread the docs will see no feedback. A one-liner warning would help:

```ts
// In analyze.ts, after the verbose check (~line 81):
if (options?.ignoreEmbeddingsNodeLimit && !options?.embeddings) {
  console.warn('  Warning: --ignore-embeddings-node-limit has no effect without --embeddings\n');
}
```

[Fix this →](https://claude.ai/code?q=In%20gitnexus%2Fsrc%2Fcli%2Fanalyze.ts%20on%20branch%20feat%2Fignore-embeddings-node-limit%2C%20after%20the%20verbose%20check%20around%20line%2081%2C%20add%20a%20warning%20if%20options%3F.ignoreEmbeddingsNodeLimit%20is%20true%20but%20options%3F.embeddings%20is%20falsy%3A%20console.warn%28%27%20%20Warning%3A%20--ignore-embeddings-node-limit%20has%20no%20effect%20without%20--embeddings%5Cn%27%29&repo=abhigyanpatwari/GitNexus)

**2. No warning about memory risk when bypassing the cap** _(low)_

The 50K limit exists to prevent OOM. When it's bypassed, the user gets no indication that they're in risky territory. Consider logging a one-time advisory after the node count is known:

```ts
// After the embeddingSkipped = false branch:
if (options.ignoreEmbeddingsNodeLimit) {
  console.warn(`  Warning: bypassing node limit (${stats.nodes.toLocaleString()} nodes) — ensure sufficient memory\n`);
}
```

**3. Reviewer's suggestion: `--embeddings [limit]`** _(design question)_

`magyargergo` suggested collapsing both flags into `--embeddings [limit]` so `--embeddings` uses the default cap and `--embeddings 0` bypasses it. This would be a cleaner UX but is a larger API change. The current two-flag approach works fine as long as the no-op case (point 1) is handled.

**4. `AnalyzeOptions` interface field placement** _(nit)_

`ignoreEmbeddingsNodeLimit` is placed at line 49 between `embeddings` and `skills`. It would read more clearly directly after `embeddings` — which it already is. This is fine as-is.

