## Overview

This PR adds GitLab support to the web client's git-clone feature. The changes are generally well-structured and the overall approach (isomorphic-git + CORS proxy + configurable host allowlist) is sound. However, there are several nuances and gaps worth addressing before merging.

---

## Critical / Security Issues

### 1. Dev proxy bypasses the allowlist — SSRF risk in development
**`vite.config.ts:16-25`**

```typescript
const isLocalDevAllowedGitHost = (url: URL): boolean => {
  if (url.protocol !== 'https:') return false;
  const configuredHosts = getConfiguredGitHostPatterns();
  if (isGitHostAllowed(url.hostname, configuredHosts)) return true;

  // Local dev should be able to talk to corporate/self-hosted Git servers
  // without forcing environment configuration first.
  return true;  // ← always returns true, allowlist is bypassed
};
```

The dev-mode proxy will proxy requests to **any** HTTPS host. This means a maliciously crafted URL (e.g. `https://internal-metadata.corp/secret`) would be proxied through the developer's machine. While the production Vercel function correctly enforces the allowlist, the dev bypass is an SSRF risk. At minimum, the bypass should be clearly documented; ideally, it could be controlled by a `GITNEXUS_DEV_ALLOW_ALL_HOSTS=1` flag instead of being unconditional.

[Fix this →](https://claude.ai/code?q=In%20gitnexus-web%2Fvite.config.ts%2C%20the%20isLocalDevAllowedGitHost%20function%20always%20returns%20true%20for%20any%20HTTPS%20host%2C%20bypassing%20the%20allowlist.%20Add%20an%20env%20flag%20GITNEXUS_DEV_ALLOW_ALL_HOSTS%20to%20control%20this%20behavior%2C%20defaulting%20to%20false%20so%20the%20allowlist%20is%20enforced%20unless%20explicitly%20opted%20out.&repo=abhigyanpatwari/GitNexus)

### 2. No request size cap in the proxy
**`proxy.ts:66-72`**, **`vite.config.ts:105-113`**

The POST body is buffered entirely into memory with no size limit. A response from a slow/malicious server could exhaust memory. isomorphic-git sends pack-protocol data which can be large for big repos. Consider adding a `maxBodyBytes` guard (e.g. 256 MB).

---

## Functional Gaps

### 3. GitLab deploy tokens not supported as a first-class use case
**`git-clone.ts:151-153`**

```typescript
if (resolvedAuthMode === 'gitlab') {
  return () => ({ username: normalizedUsername || 'oauth2', password: token });
}
```

Per [GitLab deploy token docs](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/project/deploy_tokens/#git-clone-a-repository), deploy tokens use the **token's own username** (not `oauth2`) and the token value as the password. The current UI placeholder says *"Leave empty to use oauth2 for GitLab PATs"* — but if a user has a deploy token and leaves the username empty, the clone will fail silently with a 401. Users need to be told to use `basic` mode for deploy tokens, or the `gitlab` mode description should be explicit.

According to GitLab docs the auth matrix is:
| Credential type | Username | Password |
|---|---|---|
| Personal Access Token | `oauth2` (or your username) | token value |
| OAuth2 access token | `oauth2` | token value |
| Deploy Token | deploy token username | token value |
| Job token (CI) | `gitlab-ci-token` | `$CI_JOB_TOKEN` |

### 4. Over-broad host auto-detection heuristic
**`git-clone.ts:43`**

```typescript
normalizedHost.includes('gitlab')
```

This matches any hostname containing the string `gitlab` — e.g. `notgitlab.internal`, `mygitlabfork.io`. Since `auto` is the default auth mode, users with non-GitLab hosts that happen to include `gitlab` in the name will silently get `oauth2`-style auth instead of basic auth, causing unexpected 401s. The check could be made more conservative, e.g. also requiring `normalizedHost.startsWith('gitlab.')`.

### 5. SSH URL yields a generic "Invalid URL" error
**`git-clone.ts:98-100`**

If a user pastes `git@gitlab.com:group/repo.git` (common to copy from GitLab's UI), the URL parser silently returns `null` and the error shown is the generic *"Invalid repository URL"*. isomorphic-git only supports HTTPS, so this is a valid rejection, but the message should detect SSH URLs and say *"SSH URLs are not supported. Use the HTTPS clone URL."*

[Fix this →](https://claude.ai/code?q=In%20gitnexus-web%2Fsrc%2Fservices%2Fgit-clone.ts%20parseRepositoryUrl%2C%20before%20returning%20null%2C%20detect%20if%20the%20input%20starts%20with%20git%40%20(SSH%20URL)%20and%20throw%20a%20specific%20error%20message%3A%20%22SSH%20URLs%20are%20not%20supported.%20Please%20use%20the%20HTTPS%20clone%20URL.%22&repo=abhigyanpatwari/GitNexus)

### 6. `canUseHostedGitProxy` checks the wrong hostname during cloning
**`git-clone.ts:51-55`**, **`proxy-utils.ts:47-54`**

`selectProxyBase` is called with `config.url` which is the **proxied URL** (already transformed to `/api/proxy?url=...`) on the second invocation. On the first invocation it's the raw Git target URL. Since the decision is made once when creating the HTTP client (in `createProxiedHttp`), this is fine — but if `createProxiedHttp` is ever refactored this could silently break. Passing the original `targetUrl` explicitly would make the intent clearer.

---

## Minor / UX Issues

### 7. Token flows through the hosted Vercel proxy for github.com and gitlab.com
**`git-clone.ts:51-55`**

In dev mode, private `github.com` and `gitlab.com` clones route through the hosted `gitnexus.vercel.app/api/proxy`. Credentials are embedded as HTTP Basic Auth headers and are visible to that proxy. The existing note *"Credential is used only for clone authentication and is not stored"* (`DropZone.tsx:502`) is accurate for the production path (Vercel serverless function owned by the deployer), but in dev the hosted proxy is a third-party server. This warrants a stronger disclosure, e.g. noting that in dev mode github.com/gitlab.com tokens pass through the hosted proxy.

### 8. UI auth-mode help text doesn't mention Group/Project Access Tokens
**`DropZone.tsx:382-383`**

GitLab also supports [Group Access Tokens](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/group/settings/group_access_tokens.html) and [Project Access Tokens](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/project/settings/project_access_tokens.html) (available since GitLab 14.7). These work identically to PATs (`oauth2` as username), but users may not know to select the `gitlab` auth mode for them.

### 9. `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` changes look unrelated
**`AGENTS.md`**, **`CLAUDE.md`** (+6/-6 each, same SHA `ba26bdc4`)

The diff shows 6 lines added and 6 lines removed in both files with identical SHAs. These appear to be trivial whitespace or metadata changes unrelated to the GitLab feature. They should be confirmed as intentional or reverted to reduce noise.

---

## What's Done Well

- Correctly uses `oauth2` as the default username for GitLab PATs — this matches the [GitLab PAT docs](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/user/profile/personal_access_tokens.html#clone-repository-with-personal-access-token).
- The `GITNEXUS_ALLOWED_GIT_HOSTS` env var approach for the production proxy allowlist is clean and well-documented in the error message.
- `parseRepositoryUrl` correctly handles multi-level GitLab group paths (`group/subgroup/project`).
- `www-authenticate` header is stripped by the proxy — this is important to prevent the browser's native Basic Auth popup from appearing.
- Token is cleared from React state after a successful clone.
- `proxy-utils.ts` cleanly separates the allowlist logic so it can be shared between the Vercel function and the Vite dev middleware.

