Can confirm this is a real production pain point. We run GitNexus MCP alongside automated reindex cron jobs across 25+ repos, so we hit this stale data issue multiple times per day.

## Our Current Workaround

We built a wrapper (`gni`) that handles this by killing and restarting the serve process around reindex operations:

```bash
gni_reindex() {
  local repo="$1"

  # 1. Detect and release DB lock (see #325)
  _ensure_no_lock "$repo"

  # 2. Kill serve if running (MCP connections will drop)
  local serve_pid
  serve_pid=$(lsof -ti :4747 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -n "$serve_pid" ]; then
    kill "$serve_pid" 2>/dev/null
    sleep 2
  fi

  # 3. Reindex
  gitnexus analyze --repo "$repo"

  # 4. Restart serve (fresh DB connections)
  gitnexus serve --port 4747 &
  disown

  # 5. Wait for health check
  for i in {1..10}; do
    curl -s "http://localhost:4747/health" >/dev/null 2>&1 && break
    sleep 1
  done
}
```

This works but is heavy-handed — it drops all active MCP sessions, which is disruptive when someone is mid-conversation with Claude Code.

## Agreement with Proposed Fix + Refinement

Your suggested `meta.json` mtime check in `ensureInitialized` is the right approach. A few refinements from our experience:

### Option A: `fs.stat` mtime on kuzu DB directory (simplest)

```javascript
async ensureInitialized(repoId) {
  if (this.initializedRepos.has(repoId) && isKuzuReady(repoId)) {
    const handle = this.repos.get(repoId);
    const dbDir = path.join(handle.storagePath, 'kuzu');

    // stat the DB directory — mtime changes on any write
    const stat = await fs.promises.stat(dbDir);
    const currentMtime = stat.mtimeMs;

    if (currentMtime !== handle._dbMtime) {
      console.log(`[MCP] DB changed for ${repoId} (mtime: ${handle._dbMtime} → ${currentMtime}), reconnecting...`);
      await closeKuzu(repoId);
      this.initializedRepos.delete(repoId);
      handle._dbMtime = currentMtime;
      // Fall through to re-init
    } else {
      return;
    }
  }
  // ... existing init logic
  // After init, store mtime:
  // handle._dbMtime = (await fs.promises.stat(dbDir)).mtimeMs;
}
```

Pros: No dependency on `meta.json` format, works even if meta.json isn't updated.
Cons: `fs.stat` on every query adds ~0.1ms overhead (negligible).

### Option B: File watcher (zero-overhead, event-driven)

```javascript
// On pool entry creation:
const watcher = fs.watch(dbDir, { persistent: false }, (eventType) => {
  if (eventType === 'change') {
    pool.get(repoId)._stale = true;
  }
});

// In ensureInitialized:
if (handle._stale) {
  await closeKuzu(repoId);
  // ... reconnect
  handle._stale = false;
}
```

Pros: Zero per-query overhead, instant detection.
Cons: `fs.watch` behavior varies across OS (Linux inotify vs macOS FSEvents vs Windows), and watching directories with many files can be tricky.

### Our Recommendation

**Option A (mtime check)** is the safer bet for a first fix — it's deterministic, cross-platform, and the overhead is immeasurable. Option B could be an optimization later if needed.

## Additional Edge Case

One thing we discovered: when `gitnexus analyze` runs with `--force`, it deletes and recreates the kuzu directory entirely. If the MCP server has an open file handle to the old DB at that moment, it can segfault (on Linux) or return empty results (on macOS) rather than a clean error. The reconnect logic should handle `ENOENT` / `EACCES` gracefully during the brief window when the DB is being rebuilt.

Happy to test a PR against our production setup if one is drafted.

